[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Tutorial: Accelerating Development with Firebase Extensions for Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Firebase Extensions are pre-packaged bundles of code and configuration that automate common backend tasks. By integrating extensions with Cloud Firestore, you can add powerful features to your application like resizing images, sending emails, or synchronizing data with Algolia without writing custom server-side logic from scratch."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A Firebase project with the Blaze (pay-as-you-go) billing plan enabled (required for Cloud Functions)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cloud Firestore initialized in your project."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The Firebase CLI installed and logged into your Google account."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Browsing and Selecting an Extension"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To install an extension, you can use either the Firebase Console or the Firebase CLI. For this tutorial, we will look at installing the 'Trigger Email' extension, which watches a Firestore collection and sends emails via SMTP or a transactional provider."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Installing via the Firebase CLI"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open your terminal and run the extension installation command. Replace 'firebase-ext-id' with the specific extension name, such as 'firestore-send-email'."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"firebase ext:install firestore-send-email --project=your-project-id"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The CLI will prompt you to configure parameters specific to the extension, such as your SMTP server details, the Firestore collection path to monitor (e.g., 'mail'), and sender information."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Triggering the Extension via Firestore"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once installed, the extension listens to writes in the specified collection. To trigger an action, simply write a document to the 'mail' collection using your client SDK."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"import { collection, addDoc } from "firebase/firestore";

const mailRef = collection(db, "mail");
await addDoc(mailRef, {
  to: "user@example.com",
  message: {
    subject: "Welcome to our app!",
    text: "Hello from Firebase Extensions!",
    html: "<h1>Hello from Firebase Extensions!</h1>"
  }
});"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Monitoring and Managing Extensions"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can monitor the execution logs of your installed extensions directly from the Firebase Console under the 'Extensions' dashboard. This interface also allows you to update extension versions, modify configuration parameters, or uninstall them entirely."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Firebase Extensions for Firestore dramatically reduce development time by handling routine infrastructure tasks. By leveraging pre-built modules, your team can focus on building core application features rather than managing server-side maintenance."}]
